{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 261 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "4140edb632b49c09e0ea5bc42da8c46d37286a65774af9a8aa16a528342329a6" :edb/db-before-t 260 :edb/db-after-t 261 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533573 50 #inst "2026-10-11T10:05:05.556Z" 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186171733 1071 "Disprove the following statement:\n\n$2 \\mid n^2-3$ for any $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$" 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186171733 1071 "Disprove the following statement:\n\n> $2 \\mid n^2-3$ for any $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$." 13194139533573 17592186045417 false] [17592186171733 1074 "Our statement can be written as\n\n$$\n\\forall n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\: 2 \\mid n^2-3.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\neg \\big( \\forall n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\: 2 \\mid n^2-3\\big) \\: \\equiv \\: \\exists n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\: 2 \\not\\mid n^2-3\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $n$ such that \n\n$$\n2 \\not\\mid n^2-3.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $n=0$ suffices:\n\nNotice that by substituting $n=0$ into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-3 & =(0)^{2}-3 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not divisible by $2.$ \n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $n=0$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186171733 1074 "Our statement can be written as\n$\\forall n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\: 2 \\mid n^2-3$.\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\neg \\big(\\forall n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\: 2 \\mid n^2-3\\big) \\: \\equiv \\: \\exists n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\: 2 \\not\\mid n^2-3\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\n> To disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $n$ such that\n> $2 \\not\\mid n^2-3$.\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $n=0$ suffices:\n\n> Notice that by substituting $n=0$ into the expression, we obtain\n> $n^{2} - 3|= (0)^{2} - 3; =-3$\n> which is not divisible by $2$.\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\n> So, $n=0$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." 13194139533573 17592186045417 false] [17592186171736 1071 "What would be a counterexample for the following false statement?\n\n$7n-2$ is even for any $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$" 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186171736 1071 "What would be a counterexample for the following false statement?\n\n> $7n-2$ is even for any $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$" 13194139533573 17592186045417 false] [17592186171736 1074 "Our statement can be written as\n\n![](images/da/da5d76e8b4ff04e99917f7b95ddaa8df9f8a8923ea39bcfcacb9667e88c577a0.svg)\n\nSince this statement is false, its negation is true. \n\nA *counterexample* is a particular case that disproves the statement. Equivalently, it's a case that proves the negation is true. \n\nThe negation of our statement is\n\n![](images/51/51c76ef261110759aeec7c9ffa1eb230ae4f9ae209de324ea210c0abd476a6b6.svg)\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be an integer $n$ such that $7n-2$ is odd." 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186171736 1074 "Our statement can be written as\n\n$$\n7n - 2\n$$\n\nSince this statement is false, its negation is true.\n\nA *counterexample* is a particular case that disproves the statement. Equivalently, it's a case that proves the negation is true.\n\nThe negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n7n - 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n> A counterexample for the above statement would be an integer $n$ such that $7n-2$ is odd." 13194139533573 17592186045417 false] [17592186171737 1071 "Disprove the following statement:\n\n$3q^2-2 \\gt 0$ for any $q \\in \\mathbb{Q}.$" 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186171737 1071 "Disprove the following statement:\n\n> $3q^2-2 \\gt 0$ for any $q \\in \\mathbb{Q}$." 13194139533573 17592186045417 false] [17592186171737 1074 "Our statement can be written as\n\n$$\n\\forall q \\in \\mathbb{Q}, \\: 3q^2-2 \\gt 0.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\neg \\big( \\forall q \\in \\mathbb{Q}, \\: 3q^2-2 \\gt 0 \\big) \\: \\equiv \\: \\exists q \\in \\mathbb{Q}, \\: 3q^2-2 \\leq 0\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists a rational number $q$ such that \n\n$$\n3q^2-2 \\leq 0.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $q=0$ suffices:\n\nBy substituting $q=0$ into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{q}^{2}-2 & =3{(0)}^{2}-2 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\  & \\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $q=0$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186171737 1074 "Our statement can be written as\n$\\forall q \\in \\mathbb{Q}, \\: 3q^2-2 \\gt 0$.\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\neg \\big(\\forall q \\in \\mathbb{Q}, \\: 3q^2-2 \\gt 0 \\big) \\: \\equiv \\: \\exists q \\in \\mathbb{Q}, \\: 3q^2-2 \\leq 0\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\n> To disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists a rational number $q$ such that\n> $3q^2-2 \\leq 0$.\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $q=0$ suffices:\n\n> By substituting $q=0$ into the expression, we obtain\n> $3q^{2} - 2|= 3(0)^{2} - 2; =-2; \\le 0$.\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\n> So, $q=0$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." 13194139533573 17592186045417 false] [17592186171929 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409537 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186172195 1050 420.0 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186172425 1068 #edb/ref 17592186169686 13194139533573 17592186045417 false] [17592186172425 1068 #edb/ref 17592186169691 13194139533573 17592186045417 false] [17592186172425 1068 #edb/ref 17592186169694 13194139533573 17592186045417 false] [17592186172425 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409531 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186172425 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409534 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186172425 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409536 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186172425 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186172425 1071 "Consider the following false statement.\n\n${n}^{2}-6n+8\\ge 0$ for any $n\\in N.$" 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186172425 1071 "Consider the following false statement.\n\n> $n^{2} - 6n + 8 \\ge 0$ for any $n ∈ N$.\n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be an integer $n$ such that\n$n^{2} - 6n + 8$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}}" 13194139533573 17592186045417 false] [17592186172425 1074 "Our statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀n\\in Z,\\,{n}^{2}-6n+8\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSince this statement is false, its negation is true. \n\nA *counterexample* is a particular case that disproves the statement. Equivalently, it's a case that proves the negation is true. \n\nThe negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n¬(∀n\\in Z,\\,{n}^{2}-6n+8\\ge 0)\\,≡\\,∃n\\in Z,\\,{n}^{2}-6n+8<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be an integer $n$ such that \n\n$$\n{n}^{2}-6n+8<0.\n$$" 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186173275 1064 "For example, suppose we wish to disprove the following statement:\n\n$5n-1$ is odd for any $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$ \n\nOur statement can be written using formal notation as follows:\n![](images/d2/d22dcb4aa35870e9df2417316f7de327000f9b0cbd6758f79d4552d2a144cde1.svg)\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n![](images/32/3290200527bb59151b38e031c2b4a501784ccd6ca333bc7bef22ce5096ac6fce.svg)\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\n*To disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $n$ such that $5n-1$ is even.*\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $n=1$ suffices:\n\n*Notice that by substituting $n=1$ into the expression, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5n-1 & =5(1)-1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*which is even.*\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\n*So, $n=1$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.*\n\nNow that we've figured out the details, let's write down the full argument." 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186173275 1064 "For example, suppose we wish to disprove the following statement:\n\n> $5n-1$ is odd for any $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\nOur statement can be written using formal notation as follows:\n$5n - 1$\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n$5n - 1$\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\n> *To disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $n$ such that $5n-1$ is even.*\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $n=1$ suffices:\n\n> *Notice that by substituting $n=1$ into the expression, we obtain*\n> $5n - 1|= 5(1) - 1; = 4$\n> *which is even.*\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\n> *So, $n=1$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.*\n\nNow that we've figured out the details, let's write down the full argument." 13194139533573 17592186045417 false] [17592186173297 1064 "To **disprove** a statement means to show that it is false. In this lesson, we will learn how to disprove universal statements.\n\nSuppose we wish to disprove a universal statement of the form\n\n$$\n\\forall x \\in U, \\: P(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is some universal set. To do that, we must demonstrate that the statement's negation is true.\n\nThe negation of this statement is\n\n$$\n\\neg \\big(\\forall x \\in U, \\: P(x) \\big) \\: \\equiv \\: \\exists x \\in U, \\: \\lnot P(x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, to disprove a universal statement, finding one value of the variable that makes the negation true is sufficient. This value is called a **counterexample** to the original statement." 13194139533573 17592186045417 false] [17592186173297 1064 "To **disprove** a statement means to show that it is false. In this lesson, we will learn how to disprove universal statements.\n\nSuppose we wish to disprove a universal statement of the form \n\n$$\n\\forall x \\in U, \\: P(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is some universal set. To do that, we must demonstrate that the statement's negation is true. \n\nThe negation of this statement is\n\n$$\n\\neg \\big( \\forall x \\in U, \\: P(x) \\big) \\: \\equiv \\: \\exists x \\in U, \\: \\lnot P(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, to disprove a universal statement, finding one value of the variable that makes the negation true is sufficient. This value is called a **counterexample** to the original statement." 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186173298 1064 "Disprove the following statement:\n\n*$5n-1$ is odd for any $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$*\n\nCounterexample:\n\n*To disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $n$ such that $5n-1$ is even.*\n\n*Notice that by substituting $n=1$ into the expression, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5n-1 & =5(1)-1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*which is even.*\n\n*So, $n=1$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.*" 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186173298 1064 "Disprove the following statement:\n\n> *$5n-1$ is odd for any $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.*\n\nCounterexample:\n\n> *To disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $n$ such that $5n-1$ is even.*\n> *Notice that by substituting $n=1$ into the expression, we obtain*\n> $5n - 1|= 5(1) - 1; = 4$\n> *which is even.*\n> *So, $n=1$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.*" 13194139533573 17592186045417 false] [17592186409516 1080 "even" 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186409516 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186409516 1083 #uuid "045b7395-71a2-554c-828d-fd5ca75abab2" 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186409517 1080 "9n-4" 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186409517 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186409517 1083 #uuid "46255983-63bd-5575-a696-22f606d6b1dd" 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186409518 1080 "1" 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186409518 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186409518 1083 #uuid "4760ba7c-43f4-5060-a452-2d81c2c07c7f" 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186409519 1080 ">" 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186409519 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186409519 1083 #uuid "6fd88ff2-9c21-5c96-9996-8e259a0080e2" 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186409520 1080 "n" 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186409520 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186409520 1083 #uuid "785be389-2c7e-5e32-bac1-d9b8c6fc0bda" 13194139533573 17592186045417 true] [17592186409521 1080 "{n}^{2}-6n+8" 13194139533573 17592186